 "description": "Christian wakes up inside a televised, lethal game. With no memory of how he got there, he encounters Mercer—a man who constantly flirts with him. Mercer claims to have feelings for Christian and possesses a map of the game, proposing that they team up to beat it. Though initially reluctant, Christian eventually relents after Mercer proves his worth. In reality, Mercer is a technician from Christian’s Special Forces investigative unit who entered the game specifically to find him. The two successfully clear the first round, during which Christian discovers Mercer’s true identity; however, by the end of the round, Mercer’s fate—whether he is dead or alive—remains unknown. Shortly after the second round begins, Christian realizes that the entire game world is a virtual simulation. Upon reuniting, Mercer reveals that their physical bodies are currently missing. To buy time until their teammates in the real world can locate them, they must keep up the charade—continuing their act of a flirtatious, openly gay man trying to \"turn\" a stoic, straight-laced partner. Seeking higher ratings and gambling revenue, the show’s producers deliberately try to trigger the return of Christian’s memories. Once they realize the players have discovered the virtual nature of the world, the producers turn the game into an all-out, chaotic free-for-all. Just as the virtual world begins to collapse, Christian’s memories return, and he realizes that he had feelings for Mercer even before entering the game. Working from both the inside and the outside, Christian and Mercer manage to escape the game and return to the real world. They dismantle the lethal game’s broadcast system and bring the CEO of the operating company, \"Aegis of Zeus,\" to justice in court. However, they soon discover that the company is involved in various other illicit operations.",
