 "description": "Modern-day “Dessert Queen” Emma is relentlessly pursued by her obsessive admirer Lucas. After accidentally hitting him and killing him, she transmigrates to Greeley, Colorado, in 1959, becoming Emma Walker, the town’s notorious fat woman—gluttonous, lazy, unfaithful to Harold, and squandering her husband’s workers’ compensation benefits. On the day she transmigrates, she has just sent off the lover who has moved in, only to run into Vivian, who has caught them in the act, and her heartbroken husband, Jack, who demands a divorce on the spot. Emma refuses the divorce and resolves to pay the original body’s debts and atone for its sins. When Jack is injured on the job in a cold storage room, she uses all her emergency money to pay for his treatment. But Vivian and the real thief, Doris, conspire to frame her for stealing the church bazaar proceeds, and the whole town wants her driven out. At the critical moment, Jack steps forward and takes the blame for her. Emma spends the night investigating, finds a key piece of evidence—a blue cloth strip—in the bazaar donation box, and uses the radio to launch a psychological trap, catching the real culprit, Doris, on the spot and clearing her name. Thereafter, Emma starts out with a single apple pie, moving from church bazaars to market stalls, then joining Brooks Bakery and making a name for herself. She repeatedly thwarts Vivian’s schemes head-on—such as seeing through the “I Love You” cookie box plot by the color of the icing—and wins the mayor’s order and the governor’s wife’s favor with her original “chilled dessert cups.” Harold kidnaps Emma at the train station to seize her recipes, and Jack comes to her rescue despite his injuries. Emma follows the trail and uncovers Harold’s false accounts embezzling three thousand dollars from the food factory, turns Vivian into a witness, and finally brings Harold to justice. Emma’s dessert business grows from Greeley to the state fair, then to New York department stores, earning her the title of “Dessert Queen.” She expands her workshop into a company, hires women from the town—including Doris, whom she forgives for stealing the money—and names the Thanksgiving market after herself. Jack accepts the whole truth—that she is from seventy years in the future: “No matter what you become, you are still my Emma.” In the end, they embrace amid the everyday warmth of Greeley and in the hundredth flagship store in New York—In her previous life, she had a dessert empire but was alone; in this life, she started from nothing, yet she has everything.",
