 "cover": "https://dramadog.app/c/25391.webp",
 "description": "Twenty-two-year-old Emily Parker grew up moving from one foster home to another. She put herself through college on a full scholarship and set her sights on Stanford Law School. When she learns that she was switched at birth and is the biological daughter of Seattle’s wealthy Carson family, she returns to gain access to an education trust. But her parents, Richard and Victoria, and her two older brothers, Noah and Kyle, still dote on Blair, the adopted daughter they raised. Blair acts sweet around the family, but she belittles Emily’s grades, stages a fall from a building, and locks Emily in to keep her from taking an exam. The Carsons never bother to find out what happened. They keep demanding that Emily apologize and even try to use her to secure a marriage alliance and polish the family’s public image. Emily pours her energy into the one thing she can control: her studies. She scores 179 on the Northwest practice exam, exposes Blair’s lies, and moves out of the estate. With support from Helen, Ethan, and her friends from foster care, she goes on to earn a perfect 180 on the LSAT, making headlines across the country. The Carsons try to use her success to repair their reputation, but Emily chooses to give her interview at the foster home in Tulsa. On camera, she thanks the people who actually raised her: Grace, Grandpa Parker, and the friends who grew up alongside her. In doing so, she exposes how coldly the Carsons treated her. The backlash sends Carson Group’s stock tumbling. The family leadership takes back the assets and disowns Blair, while Noah’s unhealthy attachment to Blair tears what’s left of the family apart. Three months later, Emily founds Parker Studio. Five years later, she is president of Parker Group and acquires Carson Northwest, the business her father spent his life struggling to hold on to. Even as Ethan pursues her, Emily keeps her future in her own hands. In the end, she has the confidence to live on her own terms and carry the Parker name with pride, free of any need for the Carsons’ approval.",
